<speak>
<p>Two results now meet. Delta of f is an algebraic integer, and every algebraic conjugate has absolute value one. Kronecker's theorem says that these two facts force a root of unity. To see why, call this number z, and let d be its degree over the rationals. Algebraic integrality means that z satisfies a monic polynomial with integer coefficients.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>Take any positive integer m. The power z to the m is still an algebraic integer. Its degree is at most d, and every conjugate still has absolute value one. Write its monic minimal polynomial with degree r, where r is at most d. We will bound its integer coefficients independently of m.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>Start with a schematic degree three picture. Its three roots, alpha, beta, and gamma, each have magnitude one. Expand the product of X minus each root. Up to signs, the coefficients are alpha plus beta plus gamma; alpha beta plus alpha gamma plus beta gamma; and alpha beta gamma. Every product has magnitude one. The triangle inequality bounds the three coefficient magnitudes by three, three, and one.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>In degree r, the coefficient k places below the leading term is a sum of products of k distinct roots. There are r choose k such terms, each of magnitude one. Its magnitude is at most r choose k, and hence at most d choose k. This bound does not depend on m.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>Now use the integer condition. For each possible degree, every coefficient is an integer in a bounded range. The leading coefficient is one. Only finitely many coefficient lists are possible, so only finitely many polynomials can occur. The degree bound and the coefficient bounds work for every power of z.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>Each of these finitely many polynomials has finitely many roots. Collect all their roots into one finite set. Every positive power of z belongs to this set. If the set has N elements, N plus one powers cannot all occupy different slots. Some two actual powers must agree.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>So z to the a equals z to the b for some positive integers a less than b. Since z has magnitude one, it is nonzero. Divide both sides by z to the a. We obtain z to the b minus a equals one. The exponent is positive. That is exactly the definition of a root of unity.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>Apply this to z equals Delta of f. Our two preceding results supply algebraic integrality and unit magnitude for every conjugate, so Delta has some finite positive order. We have not yet shown that the order is odd. The next node brings back the residue one information from the local comparisons to prove that further restriction.<break time="900ms"/></p>
</speak>
